================================================================================
DERIVATIVE WORK
================================================================================

This project is a DERIVATIVE of kimi-k3-in-c:

  https://github.com/FareedKhan-dev/kimi-k3-in-c
  Copyright 2026 the kimi-k3-in-c authors
  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0

Per section 4(b) of the Apache License, notice is given that files from that project
have been modified. The nature of the change is substantial: the original is a
single-architecture engine for Kimi K3, and this is a multi-architecture runtime in
which K3 is one backend among others.

WHAT WAS TAKEN, and remains upstream's work in substance:

  The techniques that let a model far larger than RAM execute at all -- weight streaming
  from storage, the routed-expert cache, prefix pinning against a cyclic layer scan,
  overlapping reads with compute, fused dot products over packed quantised weights, and
  the bit-identical scalar/vector reduction order that makes output independent of the
  memory budget. The Kimi K3 model implementation (KDA, gated MLA, Stable LatentMoE),
  its safetensors reader, its trunk packer, and its test and benchmark harnesses.

WHAT WAS CHANGED:

  Those subsystems were extracted from the K3 implementation into a model-independent
  runtime: a dtype registry and tensor abstraction, a storage layer with a format
  interface, a keyed weight cache, a pinned-prefix block streamer, a memory planner with
  hardware detection, a CPU kernel layer with runtime ISA dispatch and per-backend
  numerical policy, a quantization vtable, and a model backend interface with a
  registry. K3-specific code was moved behind that interface without changing its
  behaviour; its two determinism hashes are unchanged and gated on every commit.

WHAT WAS ADDED:

  A GGUF reader; Q4_K and Q6_K kernels; a Qwen3 backend (grouped-query attention with
  QK-norm, RoPE, dense SwiGLU); a byte-level BPE tokenizer for GGUF vocabularies; a
  multi-model command-line interface; and independent numpy reference implementations
  used to validate the above.

Upstream's README is preserved verbatim at docs/README-kimi-k3.md.

